1. YARA: The File and Memory Inspector
YARA (often described as the "Swiss Army knife for malware researchers") is used to identify and classify malware based 
on textual or binary patterns inside static files or active system memory.

Why it exists: Traditional antivirus software relies on specific file hashes (like MD5 or SHA256). If a hacker changes 
               just a single byte of code, the hash changes completely, bypassing the antivirus. YARA solves this by 
               looking for the characteristics of the code rather than the file hash.

Where it looks: Hard drives, downloaded files, email attachments, and running RAM (volatile memory).

Analogy: Think of YARA as a biometric scanner. It looks at the DNA of the file (specific strings, variables, or byte 
         sequences) to identify it, no matter what name it uses or how it masks itself.

Core Strength: Exceptional at finding specific threat actor tools, packed malware, and active exploits running in memory.

2. Sigma: The Log Standardizer
Sigma is a generic, open-source rule format used to detect malicious behavior within system and network log data.

Why it exists: Every security platform (SIEM) uses its own proprietary query language. Splunk uses SPL, Elastic uses KQL,
               Microsoft Sentinel uses KQL/ASIM. If a defender writes a rule for Splunk, it will not work in Elastic. 
               Sigma acts as a translator. You write a rule once in the universal Sigma format (YAML), and a compiler
               tool converts it automatically into a query for Splunk, Elastic, Sentinel, QRadar, or any other SIEM.

Where it looks: Windows Event Logs, Linux Syslog, EDR telemetry (Process Creation, Registry edits), Network Firewalls, 
                and Cloud Audit trails.

Analogy: Think of Sigma as a security camera log book. It doesn't look at physical files; instead, it watches what
         processes are doing (e.g., "Why did Command Prompt just open Notepad and then immediately delete a file?").

Core Strength: Exceptional at identifying behavioral anomalies, lateral movement, credential dumping, and 
               administrative abuse.
